CORE RESULT
Plasticity range should not be encoded as:

ALARM AGE
->
MORE AUTHORITY.

A better local rule is:

ALARM
+
NEIGHBOR CORROBORATION
+
PERSISTENCE

->
WIDER REPAIR FRONT.

This is the same provenance principle found elsewhere in Yggdrasil:

REPEATED CLAIMS
ARE NOT
INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE.

TWO-SITE / MULTI-FOCUS INTERPRETATION
Exploratory two-site damage tests showed:
separate local fronts can coexist.

The key desirable behavior is:

TWO LOCAL INJURIES
->
TWO LOCAL PLASTICITY MARGINS

not:

ONE ORGANISM-WIDE PLASTIC STATE.

This remains a qualitative supporting observation,
not the primary final comparator.

RELATION TO H18 / H40
H18:
multiple context messages sharing one failure mode do not create truth.

H40:
copied canaries do not create independent validation.

H76:
one persistent injury source does not earn:
larger developmental influence.

The recurring rule is:

AUTHORITY SHOULD GROW FROM:
FAILURE-MODE DIVERSITY / LOCAL CORROBORATION,

not:
raw persistence alone.

RELATION TO H75
H75 established:
bounded plasticity range is useful.

H76 adds:
the range can be generated from:
local spatial structure of the disturbance

rather than:
a globally chosen fixed radius.

OUTSIDE-STATUS-QUO IMPLICATION
A computational tissue can carry:
a moving DEVELOPMENTAL FRONT.

Cells do not need:
a global repair command.

A local injury cluster can:
grow a plastic margin
as the disturbance physically expands.

Healthy isolated tissue remains:
canalized.

DECISION
AR-H76 is POSITIVE.

Supported synthetically:

- plasticity range can depend on local spatial corroboration;
- expanding real damage can obtain the same useful repair-margin behavior as a fixed two-hop rule;
- persistence-only range expansion is vulnerable to long-lived false alarms;
- isolated false alarms remain tightly localized under coherence-gated range;
- range authority can be decentralized.

Not demonstrated:

- moving damage;
- fully asynchronous field propagation;
- globally correlated false injury;
- learned range thresholds;
- source trust for injury emitters;
- interaction with physical resource flow.

NEXT HIGH-VALUE MOVE
AR-H77 — EXCITABLE PLASTICITY WAVE / MISMATCH-GATED REPAIR FRONT

H75-H76 still represent plasticity as:
a local concentration / radius field.

A more radical alternative:

INJURY EMITS A PULSE.

The pulse propagates only through cells that detect:
LOCAL FUNCTIONAL MISMATCH
or
LOCAL DAMAGE CORROBORATION.

Cells that remain healthy/correct:
absorb the wave.

This could make repair-front range:
self-terminating
rather than:
explicitly radius-coded.

Potential advantages:

- no stored radius;
- event-triggered communication;
- front follows real functional disruption;
- healthy tissue becomes a natural boundary.

Potential failures:

- false mismatch can relay the wave;
- refractory dead zones;
- fragmented damage blocks propagation;
- pulse collision.

